#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185041} :activity/id #uuid "5c37473f-3f15-50ad-88ea-ddb970802b55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050756 :topic/id #uuid "642d830f-d131-58c7-ae56-060749a40776" :topic/math-academy-id 3811} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185359 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1bc1975-006b-5be4-ad5c-88888abb00d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6599 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "003ba853-1e82-584c-b1f2-becf3ff9c30c" :step/math-academy-id 39810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185052}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184238 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183921 :question/id #uuid "563087c2-8d87-5b95-833d-f9892d9276a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050071}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504648}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "e4ed7c3e-7162-558c-a918-39f63652e0c0" :step/math-academy-id 39811 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185044}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184239 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183922 :question/id #uuid "c1ba0b9f-78d6-5791-8816-b71da089e57f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10536"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050085}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573309}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"} :step/id #uuid "9666a609-3291-5b2c-a5f1-4c905924ddd1" :step/math-academy-id 39814 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185360 :tutorial/content "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "24cdcba9-d6c5-5719-a086-34fe417cc42f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6600 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Function at Infinity"} :step/id #uuid "66882814-9d8d-500a-b199-d60870f60cec" :step/math-academy-id 39815 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183923 :question/id #uuid "7ce46ca9-cfff-5e8b-89b9-a2a7b34921fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573268}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"} :step/id #uuid "f8205256-4d92-59b5-babd-ec4ae58b06b8" :step/math-academy-id 39823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185362 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=\\arctan x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- For every value $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arctan(x) = \\arctan(a).\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to-\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the algebra of limits to evaluate expressions involving inverse trigonometric functions. Let's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "926b00b5-d0b1-559a-9b04-c0c0636824d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6741 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "338537ec-9b29-534a-bc92-7f0375a54a6d" :step/math-academy-id 40843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185363 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\arccos(x).$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x) = \\arccos(a).\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arccos(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- The right-sided limit at $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the left-sided limit at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's deduce the limit properties of $y = \\arctan x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eea2d2ac-ea9c-551a-91c6-4ee85ab05907" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6742 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "13c5d606-b95e-5a64-ba04-2a68fbf50e14" :step/math-academy-id 40844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185051}}] :activity/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184398}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573355} :activity/id #uuid "a37052fa-fb0a-5851-826e-d84fd50e18cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050756 :topic/id #uuid "642d830f-d131-58c7-ae56-060749a40776" :topic/math-academy-id 3811} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21db6ee1-4f00-52f4-bdef-e6f8f76bdece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572866} {:answer/id #uuid "393180ce-477f-5b4c-ade3-c57dfc5fc14e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572882} {:answer/id #uuid "764e658d-447d-5f2a-93a6-3aa41f77521f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572975} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b25d82-2de9-525d-98e6-baf42b5cc386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573088} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d93958-0196-5461-ae0e-a6c55afcd4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572975} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c57c23-5852-5310-9404-decf58de586e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d1c19cde-3b7d-5f98-929b-d663820d03f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-152866" :question/problem "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2}):$ \n\n![](images/ff/fffe2ba1124b8edd04902db5ad2b0945e5e04372ae0a0ed338c893f0ae417c58.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})$ is $x\\in [2,6].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})$ is undefined."} :step/id #uuid "2ec792e1-dee9-5e77-b785-5597e0c43364" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573343}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ddabc6-6948-5d05-984f-8724d78f9060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572845} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac77e93-6655-5cb9-83e2-8d0071ffa65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572853} {:answer/id #uuid "23691b4e-ef96-59e7-b343-43a0604ca109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572868} {:answer/id #uuid "7059fd79-bfc5-56de-bb09-06241fc81d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572964} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcb3571-4895-5ea8-a376-01f2897be6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572868} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c3ab6b7-6e70-535c-9a1b-8fc6113a3975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "002cab17-2b93-5a94-bc24-04ae7b92f4f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152871" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi :$   \n\n![](images/42/42eb38a11d9d63e05c0078bae4c052566b3162571e8563f22cd52ae31bc6dbd7.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=4$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4ba79a17-62dc-5f7c-94c1-fc0884ba6fdc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d9ec78-8e28-521d-942f-9298b56ba664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272678} {:answer/id #uuid "37ff1036-5a0e-58b1-9825-df07520b3d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272877} {:answer/id #uuid "8cabb11a-cc05-5405-8952-4408c4076c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273602} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ac53fc-a437-5933-a0ee-8ea304f15e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273915} {:answer/id #uuid "f117b36f-681e-570c-bee9-72f13571afe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272678} :answer-field/id #uuid "a79374a6-9789-530a-8830-fd0dab1ccf3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "81deaa75-bb15-5048-ab19-c54290c068b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-149955" :question/problem "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1):$\n\n![](images/9e/9e2e705cf17a5dca362c23a23e4d10067ff4390a3f9464dddb6745d78986c02a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is $x\\in [0,1].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined."} :step/id #uuid "a0212770-e29d-50bd-b4a4-f099eaf2835b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573339}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cab12af0-b59f-5bad-b4db-8eea41c70809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573080} :answer-field/id #uuid "52153792-c358-5ad7-a1f9-bbbc25297261" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f397079-49a9-58d1-bc93-fa23602eefa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344566" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}cos\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/86/86c0cf22de78e3b773543680c2ace077f97b66ae822436d637c5444903e2330f.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}cos\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)) & =cos\\,(arctan\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =cos\\,(arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a502c7a6-6f04-5265-9f8d-a0ce92311190"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa045d5-9683-533e-b3b9-383180919e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315174} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c61dd80-a3fe-57a0-9d9a-a6d5e5d6f81c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b99f3255-7a91-5700-9ad5-6321b83c8397" :question/math-academy-id "q-343225" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3):$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb49c052cabe12e8869270ab61b1de9e7f45b6acd6eaa9bc0d4c6892446a7cb6.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=(10\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=5\\pi .\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ff0c03ef-8677-5c60-a9b8-6870eff0abc1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573357}}] :activity/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572822}]}
